# The Chart - an optician, a sight test, and a number that has a legal consequence.
# Written for the library. One person, one reading, and the question of who a result belongs to.

title: The Chart
start: start

== start ==
bg east_corner
music room_tone
The test takes twenty minutes and you have done four thousand of them, and the one this
morning is a man of eighty-three whose daughter made the appointment and drove him in and
is sitting in the waiting room with a magazine she is not reading.
He is cheerful. They usually are, at the start.
-> the_room

== the_room ==
You do the whole test properly because that is what it is for: the letters on the chart,
then the smaller chart, then the one with the astigmatic fan, then the field test where he
presses the button when he sees a light in the corner of his eye and does not press it
three times.
His pressures are fine. His fundus is fine. His refraction has moved a long way in two
years and the reason is cataracts, and he needs referring, and that is not the problem.
-> the_problem

== the_problem ==
The problem is the line he can read with his glasses on, which is the fifth from the
bottom, which is six over twelve, and the standard for driving is six over twelve in one
eye - so he can still read it, in the right eye, if the light is good and he takes his
time.
With both eyes open, in this room, in October, with a lamp on him, he is a line short of
where he was, and there is a form with a box on it, and the box is called VISUAL STANDARD
FOR DRIVING and the box has two options and neither of them is "nearly".
-> the_knowing

== the_knowing ==
The daughter booked it. You know that because she is in the waiting room and because the
card says so in her handwriting, and because when people book for a parent they are usually
asking a question they have not worked out how to ask out loud yet.
He has driven here. He is going to drive home. He has driven for sixty-one years and the
car is the last room of the house he is allowed to be alone in.
-> the_decision

== the_decision ==
The room is warm and the chart is on the wall and the chart does not care what anybody
wants. Your pen is over the box.
* Say it in the room, plainly, to him. -> say_it
* Tick the box that is true and say nothing about it. -> the_box
* Go and get his daughter and tell the two of them together. -> get_her
* Give him the new prescription, book him in for a year, and write in the notes. -> write_only

== say_it ==
you: Mr Ellery, I'm going to say a thing and I want you to hear all of it.
you: With both eyes you're reading a line less than you were, and the line you're reading
is the one on the form, and the form is about driving.
man: Right.
you: In the right eye, on a good day, in good light, you're over it. In this room, today,
you're not.
He is quiet for a while and then he does the thing that people of his age do, which is that
he asks you what you would do.
-> the_asked

== the_box ==
You tick the box that says not met, and you do it in ink, and it is accurate and it is
dated and it is yours, and you give him the new prescription and the referral and the
photograph of the back of his eye that he will not look at.
He says thank you and shakes your hand and goes out to the waiting room and you hear his
daughter ask him how it went, through the wall, and you hear him say the word fine.
-> ending_the_box

== get_her ==
You go out and you bring her in and you say it once, to both of them at the same time,
which is the fairer way round and which is also the harder one, because he has to hear it
in front of his child.
She asks the question before he does. She has been carrying the question since she made
the appointment, and she asks it standing up, with her coat on, which is how people ask
the thing they have actually come for.
-> the_asked_her

== the_asked_her ==
daughter: Is he all right to drive.
you: He's over the line in one eye. In this room, today, that's the answer, and it's the
answer on the form and I have to tick it.
daughter: Right.
She is quiet for a moment and then she does what she came to do, which is to start being
the person who will deal with this, and it takes about four minutes, and he watches her do
it and says nothing at all.
-> ending_in_front

== ending_in_front ==
She drives him home. He does not say anything in the car and she does not either, and the
two of them sit in it in the driveway for a minute before either gets out.
He sells the car in March. It is her that does the selling and her that takes the calls and
her that drives him to the surgery in October, and he is grateful about all of it, and he
is also a man who was told a thing in front of his child because that was judged to be the
kinder of the two ways to do it. You still think it was.
-> END

== write_only ==
You give him the new prescription and you book him in for next October and you write in
the notes what you saw, in the words the notes use, which are accurate and which nobody
will read unless something happens.
He will come back next year and you will test him again and one of the two of us will
decide something, eventually, and it will not be you and it will not be today.
-> ending_the_notes

== the_asked ==
The question is: what would you do. Which is not really a question about eyes, and which
has one honest answer and about four dishonest ones.
You have been asked it eleven times in nine years and you have never once found a way to
say it that does not sound like either permission or a sentence.
-> the_answer

== the_answer ==
you: I would drive home today, because you're over the line in one eye and you've driven
here safely and I'm not a policeman.
you: And I'd decide this week, while it's a decision, because in six months it might not
be one.
man: That's a fair answer.
you: It's a true one. It's not the same as fair.
He laughs at that, which you did not expect, and then he sits for a minute looking at the
chart on the wall, at the fifth line up from the bottom, which he can still read.
-> the_end

== the_end ==
He chooses. That is the point of saying it out loud: the choice is his and it was always
going to be his and your job was to make sure that it was a choice.
He sells the car in March. His daughter rings the practice in April to say thank you, and
you are not on the desk and the message says a man called about a car, which is the most
accurate version of what happened and the only one that is filed anywhere.
-> END

== ending_the_box ==
The form goes to the licensing people and they do what they do, which is a letter six
weeks later and a decision that is not yours and not his either, and nobody rings the
practice and nobody comes back in.
You were accurate. It is on the form, in ink, dated, and it is the most defensible thing
in this story, and it is the only thing in it that he did not get to hear from a person.
-> END

== ending_the_notes ==
He comes back the following October. He has had the cataracts done at the hospital by then
and he reads the whole chart, the bottom line, and he is delighted and he tells you about
the drive there and back, twice, and you write in the notes that the standard is met.
Which is true. Everything in the notes is true, both times, and neither entry says anything
about October, and there is nobody alive who knows about October except you, and you do not
think about it very often.
-> END
